WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.968
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.952 --> 00:00:05.310
KIAN CAPLAN: I'm Kian Caplan.

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:09.600
As a neuroscientist living
with Tourette's, OCD, and ADHD,

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.280
I study what makes
my brain different

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.260
from the average person.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:16.680
Growing up, I struggled
academically and socially.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:19.530
Paying attention, sitting
still, and waiting my turn

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:21.990
were everyday challenges
in the classroom.

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.330
And I was definitely
bullied a lot.

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:26.640
It wasn't until I
was 14 when I watched

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:30.450
a documentary about Tourette's
and realized, holy [BLEEP],,

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:32.049
I have that.

00:00:32.049 --> 00:00:35.800
My life changed the moment I
was diagnosed by a neurologist.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.950
The doctors gave me a word
for what I was experiencing,

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:41.500
but they still couldn't
explain why it was happening

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.240
or how to make it stop.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.970
Why can't I control the
movements and sounds I make?

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:49.390
Why aren't there better
treatments for people like me?

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:51.580
These are the questions
that inspired me to pursue

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:53.320
a career in neuroscience.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.950
Yes, it's been hard, but nobody
understands the experiences

00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:58.570
and challenges of
brain disorders

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:00.610
better than the people
living with them.

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:03.400
We need neurodiverse
people to study the brain

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:06.670
because our perspectives are
critical to scientific progress

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:08.350
both in the lab and society.

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:10.800
[MUSIC PLAYING]

